    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d149">

                <pb facs="0242" n="162" sameAs="frus:frus1936v01/pg162"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">500.A15A5 Submarines/11: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Chargé in the United
                        Kingdom</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Atherton</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1936-03-28T15:00:00-05:00">March 28, 1936—3
                            p.m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>104. For the Ambassador or Atherton. Davis’ 165, March 25, 10 p.m.<anchor n="6"
                        xml:id="d149-f1"/> In view of the departure of the American Delegates to the
                    Naval Conference, you are hereby authorized to sign, at such time as may be
                    determined, the procès-verbal designed to obtain further accessions to the rules
                    of international law concerning submarine warfare to which the United States as
                    well as the United Kingdom and Japan are now expressly committed under the
                    London Naval Treaty of 1930.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Hull</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
